        <!-- SECTION 1 -->

        <div class="lesson">

            <h2>1. Learn C Syntax</h2>

            <p>
                Syntax means the rules that we follow when
                writing a C program.
            </p>

            <p>
                Just like English has grammar rules,
                C programming has syntax rules.
            </p>

            <h3>Basic C Syntax</h3>

            <pre>
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main() {

    printf("Hello World");

    return 0;
}</pre>


            <h3>Important Syntax Rules</h3>

            <p>
                <b>1. Statements usually end with ;</b>
            </p>

            <pre>
printf("Hello");
int age = 19;</pre>


            <p>
                <b>2. C is case-sensitive.</b>
            </p>

            <pre>
printf("Hello");   // Correct

Printf("Hello");   // Different
</pre>


            <p>
                <b>3. Code blocks use { }.</b>
            </p>

            <pre>
if (age &gt;= 18) {

    printf("Adult");

}</pre>


            <p>
                <b>4. Text is written inside double quotes.</b>
            </p>

            <pre>
printf("Hello World");</pre>


            <div class="note">

                <b>Remember:</b>

                <p>
                    C programs are written using a specific
                    syntax. If the syntax is incorrect,
                    the compiler may show an error.
                </p>

            </div>

        </div>



        <!-- SECTION 2 -->

        <div class="lesson">

            <h2>2. C Program Structure</h2>

            <p>
                A C program has different parts.
                Understanding these parts makes it easier
                to write programs.
            </p>


            <h3>Example Structure</h3>

            <pre>
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main() {

    printf("Hello World");

    return 0;
}</pre>


            <h3>1. Header File</h3>

            <pre>
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</pre>

            <p>
                This includes the Standard Input/Output
                library. It allows us to use functions such
                as <b>printf()</b> and <b>scanf()</b>.
            </p>


            <h3>2. Main Function</h3>

            <pre>
int main()
{
    // program code
}</pre>

            <p>
                The <b>main()</b> function is where execution
                of a C program begins.
            </p>


            <h3>3. Curly Brackets</h3>

            <pre>
{
    // statements
}</pre>

            <p>
                Curly brackets define the beginning and end
                of a block of code.
            </p>


            <h3>4. Statement</h3>

            <pre>
printf("Hello World");</pre>

            <p>
                This statement tells the computer to display
                text.
            </p>


            <h3>5. Return Statement</h3>

            <pre>
return 0;</pre>

            <p>
                It indicates that the main function has
                finished successfully.
            </p>

        </div>



        <!-- SECTION 3 -->

        <div class="lesson">

            <h2>3. Your First C Program</h2>

            <p>
                Let's create your first C program.
            </p>


            <h3>Step 1 — Write the Code</h3>

            <pre>
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main() {

    printf("Hello World");

    return 0;
}</pre>


            <h3>Step 2 — Understand the Code</h3>

            <p>
                <b>#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;</b>
                → Includes the standard input/output library.
            </p>

            <p>
                <b>int main()</b>
                → Starting point of the program.
            </p>

            <p>
                <b>printf()</b>
                → Displays text on the screen.
            </p>

            <p>
                <b>return 0;</b>
                → Ends the program successfully.
            </p>


            <h3>Step 3 — Output</h3>

            <pre class="output">
Hello World</pre>


            <div class="note">

                <b>Beginner Practice:</b>

                <p>
                    Change "Hello World" to:
                </p>

                <pre>
Welcome to C Programming!</pre>

            </div>

        </div>


